### Summary

- add an explicit automation workspace with separate read and write directory capabilities;
- require relative forward-slash request paths below configured roots;
- reject absolute, traversing, malformed, and unsupported Windows path forms before I/O;
- keep normal desktop file dialogs and explicit one-shot local CLI operations separate;
- fail closed for automation commands that still depend on ambient filesystem paths;
- add hostile-input, permission-separation, absent-output, and link-escape regression coverage;
- document the implemented boundary for desktop control, CLI server, and MCP use.

### Configuration

Long-lived automation accepts `--automation-read-root` and
`--automation-write-root`, with equivalent
`PHOTOCRAFT_AUTOMATION_READ_ROOT` and `PHOTOCRAFT_AUTOMATION_WRITE_ROOT`
environment variables. Missing authority fails closed.

### Verification

- automation/UI and CLI/desktop tests pass;
- relevant Clippy is clean apart from three pre-existing untouched
  `collapsible_match` findings;
- dependency layers and wasm checks pass;
- mdBook build/test and relative/external-link validation pass;
- release desktop/CLI builds pass;
- a bounded synthetic before/after replay confirms outside-root access is
  blocked while in-root read/write remains functional.

### Scope

This is intentionally a focused filesystem-policy change. It does not add the
broader per-method command-capability model, which should be reviewed separately.

### Limitations

- configured roots are trusted operator choices;
- hard links already present beneath a root are not distinguished from normal files;
- the Windows junction regression passes; the symlink case runs when the host
  permits test link creation.

No private reproduction artifacts or tokens are included in this pull request.

Select › Color Range is one of the most used selection tools in Photoshop, but `select.colorRange` only covered one case of its dialog: a single sampled colour with Fuzziness. The dialog's **Select** menu (Reds … Magentas, Highlights / Midtones / Shadows, Out of Gamut), the **Add to Sample** eyedropper (several colours), **Localized Color Clusters** with its Range slider, and **Invert** were missing, so "select the sky's blues", "select the shadows" or "select what won't print" weren't possible from the engine, the CLI or MCP. In the app, Select › Color Range… ran immediately with the foreground colour instead of opening a dialog.

### What changed
- **algo (`selection`):** `color_range_samples` (best match over several samples, each judged exactly like the existing `color_range`; optional spatial falloff from where each sample was picked for Localized Color Clusters), `hue_range` (hue families) and `tone_range` (tonal ranges). The single-colour `color_range` is unchanged and still used by Replace Color.
- **engine:** `select.colorRange` gains
  - `select`: `sampledColors` (default, previous behaviour) | `reds` `yellows` `greens` `cyans` `blues` `magentas` | `highlights` `midtones` `shadows` | `outOfGamut`;
  - `colors: [...]` and `points: [[x, y], …]` (eyedropper samples taken from the sampled pixels; combined with `color` when given);
  - `subtractPoints: [[x, y], …]` (the Subtract from Sample eyedropper: pixels matching a subtracted sample, with the same Fuzziness, drop out of the range);
  - `localized` + `range` (0–100 % of the canvas's longer side) for Localized Color Clusters (needs `points`);
  - `tonalRange` (a level for Shadows / Highlights, `[low, high]` for Midtones) with `fuzziness` as a percentage of the tonal scale, like the dialog;
  - `invert`.

  `mode` (replace/add/subtract/intersect) and `sampleAllLayers` work as before; one call is one undo step. Unknown presets, points outside the canvas, malformed lists, `localized` without points and bad to…

Likely fix for #375: a 4.75 GB PSB fails to open with "The parameter is incorrect. (os error 87)". I couldn't reproduce that error, so this refers to the issue rather than closing it. It also makes File › Open report read errors instead of silently doing nothing.

## Cause

- **Every open path reads the whole file with `std::fs::read`.** That asks Windows for up to 4 GiB in a single `ReadFile`, and some file systems (exFAT, network redirectors) report a read that large as `ERROR_INVALID_PARAMETER` (os error 87) ([Raymond Chen on `nNumberOfBytesToRead`](https://devblogs.microsoft.com/oldnewthing/20250804-00?p=111432)).
- **Not reproduced here.** On local NTFS both readers read a 4.75 GB file fine. My best explanation is the reporter's volume. @HectorZaGa, which kind of drive is the file on? Testing a build of this branch would confirm it.

## Change

- **`photocraft_format::read_file` / `read_all`** (`crates/format/src/read.rs`, beside `atomic_write`):
  - reads in bounded 64 MiB reads;
  - reserves the buffer up front, and a file too large for memory gets an `OutOfMemory` error that says how much it needed ("not enough memory to read the whole file (4.75 GB)");
  - speed is unchanged: 1.11 s against 1.13 s for a warm 4.75 GB file.
- **Every document read goes through it**, found by grepping every non-test `fs::read` / `read_to_end` on current `main`:
  - the background open job (`engine/src/jobs.rs` `read_path`, the desktop default);
  - the engine's `read_file`;
  - `open_path` (Open Recent, OS open events);
  - desktop drag and drop, which now calls `open_path` and so also uses the background job when jobs are on;
  - the File › Open dialog;
  - `.pcraft` loading (`format::load_path_with`);
  - `automation::files::open` and the capability-scoped workspace read;
  - smart object Replace Contents and linked files.
- **Left as they are:** reads of small files (ICC profiles, fonts, scripts, presets, notes, displacement maps) and `photocraft-psd`'s `PsdFile::open`. Tha…

Fixes #593

## Cause

On Wayland a dock icon has exactly one source: the compositor takes the window's `app_id` (`ai.storyteller.photocraft` — already set correctly via `ViewportBuilder::with_app_id` → egui-winit `with_name` → winit `set_app_id`) and resolves it against a **host-installed** `.desktop` file. An AppImage installs nothing, so every run falls back to the generic Wayland logo, as in the screenshot.

The app cannot fix this client-side on our stack: winit 0.30.13 (egui 0.36.2's pin) has an empty `set_window_icon` on Wayland, and the xdg-toplevel-icon-v1 implementation (winit PR #4288) only exists on the 0.31 line (`0.31.0-beta.3`), with partial compositor support (Plasma 6.3+). deb/rpm/Flatpak installs were never affected.

## Change

- `packaging/linux/AppRun` is now a script instead of a symlink to the binary. Before launching it does best-effort desktop integration into `$XDG_DATA_HOME`:
  - installs `applications/ai.storyteller.photocraft.desktop` from the packaged entry with `Exec` rewritten to the AppImage's absolute path (quoted; the binary isn't on the host PATH) and `TryExec` dropped (it would hide the entry); name, MIME types and categories stay as shipped;
  - copies the hicolor icons next to it;
  - everything is content-compared, so an unchanged install costs a handful of `cmp` calls and rewrites nothing (verified by inode in the test); moving the AppImage refreshes `Exec`;
  - `PHOTOCRAFT_NO_DESKTOP_INTEGRATION=1` opts out; without `$APPIMAGE` (an extracted AppDir) it does nothing;
  - **no failure can block the launch** — every step is guarded and silent, a readonly `$HOME` just skips integration.
- `packaging/linux/apprun-test.sh`: 8-case smoke test — argument passthrough, `Exec` rewrite (path with spaces), metadata preserved, idempotence, move refresh, opt-out, unwritable data dir, extracted AppDir. `desktop-file-validate` runs on the generated entry when available.
