Fixes #1011.

**What the user saw.** docs/plugins.md says a plug-in's `name` (its menu label) must be "1–64 printable characters". `Manifest::validate` only rejected control characters. So a name with a right-to-left override (U+202E), a soft hyphen (U+00AD) or only zero-width spaces (U+200B) still installed. That name then appeared unchanged as the Filter › Plug-ins menu label, the dialog title, the history label and the command description.

**What changed.** `Manifest::validate` (crates/plugins/src/manifest.rs) now rejects a `name` that contains any Unicode format character (general category Cf, Unicode 15.1). This covers bidi overrides, embeddings, isolates and marks, zero-width spaces, the BOM and the soft hyphen. The zero-width joiner and non-joiner are the exceptions, because Persian, Indic scripts and emoji sequences need them and they cannot reorder text. A name must also contain at least one visible character: one that is not whitespace, not a control character, not Cf, and not a default-ignorable filler or variation selector. Existing behaviour stays the same:
- the 64-character limit counts characters, not bytes;
- control characters are still rejected;
- the error message is the same.

docs/plugins.md now states the rule.

**Why this scope.** Only `name` changed. It is the field the issue reports, and it is the one shown as the menu label, dialog title, history label and command description. Parameter keys are already ASCII-only. Choice options, description and author are a separate surface and are not changed here.

**Tests.** The new test `name_must_be_printable_unicode` rejects:
- the three samples from the issue;
- bidi isolates, RLM and a leading BOM;
- a lone ZWJ;
- a Hangul filler with a variation selector;
- whitespace only;
- a control character.

It checks that these names still parse unchanged: "Tone…", "Kontrast ±", Persian with ZWNJ, a ZWJ emoji sequence, ❤️ with VS16, and CJK. It also checks that 64 two-byte characters pass and 65 fail. Th…

This updates PhotoCraft’s macOS window chrome to use Liquid Glass traffic-light styling on macOS 27+, while preserving existing behavior on older macOS versions. The change is runtime-gated by OS major version to avoid affecting unsupported platforms.

- **Problem addressed**
  - The window controls (close/minimize/expand traffic lights) did not adopt the Liquid Glass appearance on newer macOS releases.

- **Window styling implementation (macOS-only)**
  - Added `apply_liquid_glass_traffic_lights()` in `apps/photocraft/src/mac_window.rs`.
  - Applies AppKit titlebar/window style updates to existing app windows:
    - transparent titlebar
    - hidden title text
    - unified compact toolbar style
    - removed titlebar separator

- **Runtime compatibility gate**
  - Added OS-version check via `NSProcessInfo::operatingSystemVersion()`.
  - Liquid Glass styling is applied only when `majorVersion >= 27`.

- **Startup wiring**
  - Invoked the new styling function during app startup in `apps/photocraft/src/main.rs` (macOS path), alongside existing native title-drag configuration.

- **Dependency feature update**
  - Enabled `NSProcessInfo` in macOS `objc2-foundation` features in `apps/photocraft/Cargo.toml`.

- **Focused logic coverage**
  - Added a small unit test for the version gate (`26 -> false`, `27 -> true`).

```rust
pub fn apply_liquid_glass_traffic_lights() {
    if !liquid_glass_supported(macos_major_version()) {
        return;
    }
    for window in NSApplication::sharedApplication(mtm).windows().iter() {
        window.setTitlebarAppearsTransparent(true);
        window.setTitleVisibility(NSWindowTitleVisibility::Hidden);
        window.setToolbarStyle(NSWindowToolbarStyle::UnifiedCompact);
        window.setTitlebarSeparatorStyle(NSTitlebarSeparatorStyle::None);
    }
}
```

Fixes #1658, Fixes #1659.

On many Linux desktops, Alt+drag moves the window: **Cinnamon (Linux Mint's default), MATE, Xfce, and KDE before Plasma 6** grab every Alt+left press, so the click never reaches PhotoCraft. On a Brush the ⌥ Eyedropper samples nothing and the Clone Stamp gets no source point, while the ⌥ cursor still shows (the keyboard isn't grabbed until the press) — so it looks like a PhotoCraft bug.

The app can't win that grab back, but the grab has a clear signature (X11 and Wayland report it as the pointer leaving the window): the pointer "leaves" from well inside the canvas while ⌥ is held on a tool whose ⌥-click matters, and comes back to a still-focused window. This change watches for it and shows a status-bar message saying what happened and how to get ⌥-click back.

What's included:

- **`alt_grab.rs`** — per-view watch that arms on the physical ⌥ key over the canvas and reports a grabbed ⌥-click in the status bar (a latched Window › Modifier Keys ⌥ is not a held key and never triggers it; Alt+Tab away leaves the window unfocused and says nothing).
- **New tests** (real canvas via `egui_kittest`, driven like the control channel):
  - ⌥-click with the Brush samples the foreground and paints nothing;
  - ⌥-click sets the Clone Stamp source and a drag paints it;
  - Clone Stamp paints an empty top layer for `Sample: All Layers` and `Current & Below`, aligned and unaligned;
  - a window-manager grab reports the message for Brush, Pencil, Clone Stamp and Healing;
  - Alt+Tab, plain clicks and a real ⌥-click say nothing;
  - a latched Alt (Window › Modifier Keys) samples and sets the source without the key.
- **i18n** for the new message in every language.

Closes #2043.

Edit › Auto-Blend Layers › Stack Images with Seamless Tones and Colors now builds the merged layer by fusing the selected layers on the Laplacian pyramid instead of blending the per-pixel masks with a multiresolution spline.

**Algorithm** (`photocraft_algo::pyramid::fuse_stack`): the multifocus composite of Adelson, Anderson, Bergen, Burt & Ogden, *Pyramid methods in image processing* (RCA Engineer 1984) with the selection rules of Wang & Chang, *A Multi-focus Image Fusion Method Based on Laplacian Pyramid* (J. Computers 6(12), 2011), generalised to N layers:
- band-pass levels: per coefficient, the layer with the largest region energy (3×3 binomial window of the luminance coefficient squared) wins; ties keep the earlier layer; a layer has no say where its coverage is below ½;
- residual: local deviation + local entropy over 5×5, as a Pareto rule (the mean of the non-dominated layers);
- halo control (`haloControl`, 0 = off … 8, default 2): the levels coarser than the guide (quarter resolution) are the mean of the layers weighed by the guide's region energy to that power, REDUCEd to each level's size, so a bright object's defocused glow in the other layers stays out of the coarse levels;
- layers are folded in one at a time (running pyramid + one best-energy / weight-sum plane per level), any colour model and depth, `par_rows` (single-threaded on wasm), no panics.

The masks stay what they were (the per-pixel pick shown in the Layers panel); only the merged layer changes, and only for `method: "stack"`. Panorama is untouched. `pyramid::blend` stays for other callers.

The engine rejects a `haloControl` that is not a number in 0..=8. `haloControl` is not a Photoshop option: it is deliberately exposed as PhotoCraft's own control, because the halo problem (a bright object's defocused glow bleeding into the coarse levels) is what users of focus stacking complain about most and no fixed setting fits every stack. The generic dialog picks it up from the par…

Closes #2067.

`edit.autoBlendLayers` with `method: "stack"` gets `depthMap: true`, which adds a hidden gray **Depth** layer — the layer each pixel is sharpest in, black = the bottom layer, white = the top — by depth from focus over the aligned layers. Hidden so the composite stays the merge; copied onto a layer's mask it drives Filter › Blur › Lens Blur (`depthMap: layerMask`). The result JSON gains `"depth": <layer id>`. Panorama + `depthMap` is rejected.

**Algorithm** (`photocraft_algo::dff`), the non-learned focus-volume recipe:
1. per-layer focus measure on luminance: the ring difference filter of Jeon, Surh, Im & Kweon (IEEE TIP 2019), block-averaged to a half-resolution working grid;
2. cost aggregation of every slice with the guided filter (He, Sun & Tang, PAMI 2013), the merged all-in-focus luminance as guide (the per-pixel pick's luminance without Seamless Tones);
3. a streaming per-pixel peak search over the layer axis with the Gaussian interpolation of Nayar & Nakagawa (PAMI 1994) for sub-layer depth, and a confidence from the peak's prominence, its ratio to the runner-up and a noise gate; a 3×3 median;
4. edge-aware weighted least squares (Farbman et al., SIGGRAPH 2008) with the confidence as data term, solved by conjugate gradients preconditioned by a multigrid V-cycle (2×2 aggregation, damped Jacobi) from the fast global smoother's separable sweeps (Min et al., IEEE TIP 2014), with one Huber reweighting so isolated outliers stop pulling;
5. guided upsampling to full resolution.

Slices are folded one at a time (memory: one working grid per layer plus a few planes); `par_rows`/`par_map` (single-threaded on wasm); the column sweeps run on transposed planes, so no raw-pointer column blocks; no panics; deterministic. `depth_from_focus` is public and checks its input: without layers, or when `guide` or a plane is not `w × h` long, it returns the empty map (depth 0, confidence 0) before reading anything.

